# interplay_lifetime_heap_deleted_under_buffer: A heap deleted (37) while a buffer placed in it lives: the heap buffer keeps the old heap's Metal heap (the heap buffer takes only its MTLHeap), and the next exec that lists the heap makes a new one from the record. In-process the new heap is paged from the guest pages, which never saw the old heap buffer's fill: a new heap buffer placed at the same offset reads the guest bytes while the old one still reads its fill, and synchronizing the new heap (flags 6) writes the guest's own bytes back, so the old buffer's later fill never reaches the guest. In the service both heaps are detach heaps over the same guest pages, so both buffers see one memory and every fill is in the guest pages.
